#### Java applets

An **applet** is a small Java program that is embedded in a web page (HTML) and run inside a browser (or the `appletviewer`) under the control of the Java plug-in. It extends `java.applet.Applet` (or `javax.swing.JApplet`). Applets have no `main()`; the browser/appletviewer calls their lifecycle methods. For security they run in a sandbox: they cannot read or write local files, run native programs, or connect to hosts other than the one they came from. (Applets are deprecated in modern Java, but are part of the syllabus.)

**Applet life cycle methods**

| Method | When called / purpose |
|---|---|
| `init()` | Called once when the applet is first loaded; initialises variables, loads images, sets up the GUI. |
| `start()` | Called after `init()` and each time the page is revisited; starts threads/animation. |
| `paint(Graphics g)` | Called whenever the applet window needs drawing (first display, resize, `repaint()`). |
| `stop()` | Called when the page is left or minimised; suspends work. |
| `destroy()` | Called once when the applet is unloaded; releases resources. |

Order: `init() -> start() -> paint() -> stop() -> (start() again) -> destroy()`.

**Running:** compile with `javac`, embed using an `<applet>` tag (or run via appletviewer):

```html
<applet code="ShapesApplet.class" width="400" height="300"></applet>
```

**Example applet using four Graphics functions** (`drawString`, `drawLine`, `drawRect`, `fillOval`):

```java
import java.applet.Applet;
import java.awt.Graphics;
import java.awt.Color;

/* <applet code="ShapesApplet" width="400" height="300"></applet> */
public class ShapesApplet extends Applet {
    public void paint(Graphics g) {
        g.setColor(Color.BLUE);
        g.drawString("Java Applet Demo", 140, 30);   // text
        g.setColor(Color.RED);
        g.drawLine(20, 50, 380, 50);                 // line
        g.setColor(Color.GREEN);
        g.drawRect(50, 80, 120, 80);                 // rectangle outline
        g.setColor(Color.MAGENTA);
        g.fillOval(220, 80, 120, 80);                // filled ellipse
    }
}
```

Run with `appletviewer ShapesApplet.java`. The output shows a title, a horizontal red line, a green rectangle and a filled magenta oval.

**Exception:** Exceptions are run-time anomalies or unusual conditions that a program may encounter while executing. They do not include logic or syntax errors, but anomalies such as division by 0 or running out of disk space.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class MyException {
    int kg;
public:
    MyException(int wt) { kg = wt; }

    void what() {
        try {
            if (kg <= 100) {
                cout << kg << endl;
            } else {
                throw kg;
            }
        }
        catch (int kg) {
            cout << "weight is over 100kgs";
        }
    }
};
```

There can be any number of catch blocks, one for each different type of exception.

If a catch block cannot handle the particular exception it has caught, it can rethrow the exception so that an outer handler deals with it. Rethrowing causes the originally thrown object to be thrown again.

```cpp
try {
    // code that may throw
}
catch (Exception ex) {
    // partial handling
    throw;   // rethrow the original exception
}
```

