Approach: forward-declare `mechanical_student`; each class declares the other as a `friend class`, so its `show()` can read the other class's private data. A non-member friend function `add()` is declared a friend in both classes to add their marks.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

class mechanical_student;               // forward declaration

class IT_student {
    string name;
    int age, marks, roll;               // private
public:
    void get() {
        cout << "Enter IT student name, age, marks, roll: ";
        cin >> name >> age >> marks >> roll;
    }
    void show(mechanical_student &m);   // defined after both classes
    friend class mechanical_student;    // (ii) mechanical_student is friend of IT_student
    friend void add(IT_student &, mechanical_student &);   // (i) friend function
};

class mechanical_student {
    string name;
    int age, marks, roll;               // private
public:
    void get() {
        cout << "Enter Mechanical student name, age, marks, roll: ";
        cin >> name >> age >> marks >> roll;
    }
    void show(IT_student &i);
    friend class IT_student;            // (ii) IT_student is friend of mechanical_student
    friend void add(IT_student &, mechanical_student &);   // (i)
};

// IT's show(): own details first, then mechanical student's details
void IT_student::show(mechanical_student &m) {
    cout << "\n--- IT student ---\n";
    cout << "Name: " << name << " Age: " << age << " Marks: " << marks << " Roll: " << roll << endl;
    cout << "--- Mechanical student ---\n";
    cout << "Name: " << m.name << " Age: " << m.age << " Marks: " << m.marks << " Roll: " << m.roll << endl;
}

// Mechanical's show(): own details first, then IT student's details
void mechanical_student::show(IT_student &i) {
    cout << "\n--- Mechanical student ---\n";
    cout << "Name: " << name << " Age: " << age << " Marks: " << marks << " Roll: " << roll << endl;
    cout << "--- IT student ---\n";
    cout << "Name: " << i.name << " Age: " << i.age << " Marks: " << i.marks << " Roll: " << i.roll << endl;
}

// (i) friend function adding marks of both students
void add(IT_student &i, mechanical_student &m) {
    cout << "\nTotal marks (IT + Mech) = " << i.marks + m.marks << endl;
}

int main() {
    IT_student it;
    mechanical_student me;
    it.get();
    me.get();
    it.show(me);     // IT first, then Mech
    me.show(it);     // Mech first, then IT
    add(it, me);
    return 0;
}
```

Key points: friendship is not symmetric or inherited, so each class must name the other as friend explicitly; `show()` bodies are defined after both class definitions because they access members of the other (then-complete) class.

#### Packages in Java

A **package** is a namespace that groups related classes, interfaces and sub-packages into one unit. It is similar to a folder.

**Advantages**
- Organises code and avoids name conflicts (two classes with the same name can live in different packages).
- Provides access protection (`protected` and default/package-private access).
- Promotes reusability and easier maintenance.

**Types**
- *Built-in packages:* `java.lang` (auto-imported), `java.util`, `java.io`, `java.awt`, `java.sql`, etc.
- *User-defined packages:* created with the `package` statement.

**Creating a package**
1. The first statement of the source file is `package packageName;`.
2. Compile with `javac -d . File.java`, which creates a folder matching the package name.
3. Use the classes with `import packageName.ClassName;` (or `import packageName.*;`) or the fully qualified name.

**Example**

File `mypack/Calc.java`:
```java
package mypack;

public class Calc {
    public int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }
}
```

File `Test.java`:
```java
import mypack.Calc;

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Calc c = new Calc();
        System.out.println("Sum = " + c.add(10, 20));
    }
}
```

Commands:
```
javac -d . mypack/Calc.java
javac Test.java
java Test
```
Output: `Sum = 30`

The `CLASSPATH` (or `-cp`) tells the JVM where to find the package folders. Only `public` classes/members are accessible from other packages.

#### Templates in C++

A **template** is a mechanism for **generic programming**: a single definition works for any data type, and the compiler generates the type-specific code (instantiation) at compile time. It is a form of compile-time polymorphism and removes duplicated code for different types.

Syntax: `template <class T>` (or `template <typename T>`).

**Categories**

**1. Function templates** - a generic function.
```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

template <class T>
T maxOf(T a, T b) {
    return (a > b) ? a : b;
}

int main() {
    cout << maxOf(3, 7) << endl;        // T = int      -> 7
    cout << maxOf(2.5, 1.5) << endl;    // T = double   -> 2.5
    cout << maxOf('a', 'z') << endl;    // T = char     -> z
}
```

**2. Class templates** - a generic class whose data members/functions use type parameters.
```cpp
template <class T>
class Stack {
    T arr[50];
    int top;
public:
    Stack() : top(-1) {}
    void push(T x) { arr[++top] = x; }
    T pop() { return arr[top--]; }
    bool empty() { return top == -1; }
};

int main() {
    Stack<int> s1;      s1.push(10); s1.push(20);
    Stack<string> s2;   s2.push("hi");
}
```

**Further forms (variations)**
- *Multiple type parameters:* `template <class T1, class T2> class Pair { T1 a; T2 b; };`
- *Non-type parameters:* `template <class T, int N> class Array { T a[N]; };`
- *Template specialization:* a special definition for a particular type, e.g. `template <> const char* maxOf(const char* a, const char* b) {...}`.
- *Member function templates* and *overloading of function templates* are also allowed.

**Advantages:** code reuse, type safety, no loss of efficiency (resolved at compile time). The standard template library (vector, list, map, ...) is built on class templates.

#### Run-time polymorphism

**Run-time (dynamic) polymorphism** means the function to be executed is decided **at run time** according to the type of the object actually pointed to, not the type of the pointer/reference. In C++ it is implemented using **virtual functions** and **function overriding** with a base-class pointer or reference; in Java all non-static, non-final, non-private instance methods are virtual by default.

**Requirements**
1. Inheritance (base and derived classes).
2. A function in the base class declared `virtual`.
3. The derived class overrides it with the same signature.
4. The call is made through a base-class pointer or reference.

**Mechanism (vtable):** each class with virtual functions has a table of function pointers (vtable); each object holds a hidden pointer (vptr) to its class's vtable. A virtual call looks up the function through the vptr, which is why it is called *late/dynamic binding*.

**Example**
```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Shape {
public:
    virtual void draw() { cout << "Drawing shape\n"; }
    virtual ~Shape() {}
};
class Circle : public Shape {
public:
    void draw() { cout << "Drawing circle\n"; }
};
class Square : public Shape {
public:
    void draw() { cout << "Drawing square\n"; }
};

int main() {
    Shape *p;
    Circle c;  Square s;
    p = &c;  p->draw();   // Drawing circle
    p = &s;  p->draw();   // Drawing square
}
```
Without `virtual`, both calls would print "Drawing shape" (static binding).

**Related ideas:** pure virtual functions and abstract classes define interfaces; a virtual destructor is needed when deleting through a base pointer.

**Compile-time vs run-time:** compile-time polymorphism (overloading, templates) is resolved by the compiler; run-time polymorphism is flexible and extensible but has a small vtable overhead.

