Templates in C++ are a blueprint or formula for creating generic classes and functions.

Using them, we can pass data types as a parameter, so we do not need to write the same code for different data types.

**Class template:**

```cpp
template <class T>
class A {
    T* a;
};
```

**Function template:**

```cpp
template <typename T>
T add(T a, T b) {
    return a + b;
}
```

**Late binding** (also called dynamic or run-time polymorphism) is the situation where a function call is resolved at run time.

It is achieved using function overriding, which happens when a derived class has a member function with the same signature as the base class.

```cpp
class Parent {
public:
    void print() { /* ... */ }
};

class Child : public Parent {
public:
    void print() { /* ... */ }
};
```

| If the parent function is normal, only the parent's version executes when called through a parent pointer/reference to a child object. | If the parent function is virtual, the child's version executes when called through a parent pointer/reference to a child object. |
|---|---|
